# Trivia — Onion or Not?
# kind: trivia

Headline: "World Death Rate Holding Steady At 100 Percent"
---
The Onion
~ Real newspaper

Headline: "Headless Body In Topless Bar"
---
Real newspaper
~ The Onion
---
New York Post, April 1983, about a Queens bar murder. Probably the most famous tabloid headline ever written.

Headline: "CIA Realizes It's Been Using Black Highlighters All These Years"
---
The Onion
~ Real newspaper

Headline: "Sticks Nix Hick Pix"
---
Real newspaper
~ The Onion
---
Variety, 1935: rural audiences ("sticks") reject ("nix") movies about rural life ("hick pix").

Headline: "Archaeologists Discover Ancient Race Of Skeleton People"
---
The Onion
~ Real newspaper

Headline: "Super Caley Go Ballistic, Celtic Are Atrocious"
---
Real newspaper
~ The Onion
---
The Sun, 2000, after tiny Inverness Caledonian Thistle knocked Celtic out of the Scottish Cup. Sung to Mary Poppins.

Headline: "Drugs Win Drug War"
---
The Onion
~ Real newspaper

Headline: "Foot Heads Arms Body"
---
Real newspaper
~ The Onion
---
About Labour's Michael Foot being named to lead a nuclear disarmament committee. Widely attributed to The Times, possibly embellished in the retelling — the pun was too good to check.

Headline: "Rotation Of Earth Plunges Entire North American Continent Into Darkness"
---
The Onion
~ Real newspaper

Headline: "Freddie Starr Ate My Hamster"
---
Real newspaper
~ The Onion
---
The Sun, 1986. The comedian never ate the hamster — the story came from a publicist — but it became the most famous British tabloid headline of all.

Headline: "Study: Dolphins Not So Intelligent On Land"
---
The Onion
~ Real newspaper

Headline: "Ford To City: Drop Dead"
---
Real newspaper
~ The Onion
---
New York Daily News, 1975, after President Ford vowed to veto a bailout of nearly-bankrupt New York City. Ford never said the words, and losing New York in 1976 may have cost him the election.

Headline: "Man Who Thought He'd Lost All Hope Loses Last Additional Bit Of Hope He Didn't Even Know He Still Had"
---
The Onion
~ Real newspaper

Headline: "Wall St. Lays An Egg"
---
Real newspaper
~ The Onion
---
Variety on the 1929 stock market crash — showbiz slang for a flop. The companion piece to Sticks Nix Hick Pix.
